Looking only at the declaration loses recursion introduced through type arguments. In this example, `a -> T` is classified as `Simple` even when `T` is `S[int]` or `D[int]`, so it runs before the incompatible finite overloaded `z` member. Comparing `a` then expands the recursive overloads unnecessarily.

On the following reproducer, main reports `invalid-return-type` in 0.034 seconds, while this PR hangs.

Could we recognize recursion introduced by specialization while keeping already-recursive declarations lazy?

