body:
My understanding is that the main issue is determining what to assert at the end of the test, not setting up the test server initially. There's no user-visible output that we're using one backend or the other (except the new logging call). So it makes it hard to assert something useful in the e2e tests. But you'd know better about our e2e test capabilities than me, I could certainly be missing something.

Codex started producing something like this when I asked for an e2e test, which is what I thought looked too janky to include:

```rust
fn without_uv(body: impl FnOnce() -> Result<()>) -> Result<()> {
    const CHILD: &str = "RUFF_TEST_ISOLATED_CHILD";
    let thread = std::thread::current();
    let name = thread.name().context("missing test name")?;
    if std::env::var(CHILD).as_deref() == Ok(name) {
        return body();
    }
    let empty_path = tempfile::tempdir()?;
    let output = Command::new(std::env::current_exe()?)
        .args(["--exact", name, "--nocapture"])
        .env(CHILD, name)
        .env("PATH", empty_path.path())
        .current_dir(empty_path.path())
        .output()?;
    ensure!(output.status.success(), "{}\n{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout), String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stderr));
    println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout));
    Ok(())
}
